#heading(level: 1)[Starting a New Modded Game]

This chapter walks through setting up your first campaign with the full modlist.

#heading(level: 2)[Which Mods to Enable]

For your first modded run, enable these categories:

- *Essential:* Bugfixes, Performance Tools, UI/HUD
- *Recommended:* Culture & Religion, Events & Decisions, Gameplay Mechanics, Map Expansion
- *Optional (safe):* Map & Environment, Portraits & Models, Music & Sound
- *Save for later:* Fantasy, Adult

#heading(level: 2)[Game Rules Setup]

CK3's game rules let you customize your experience. With the modlist, pay attention to:

- *Cultural conversion speed:* Set to slower for more stable runs, faster for conquest-heavy runs
- *AI marriage logic:* Mods like More Interactive Vassals change AI marriage behavior
- *Disease frequency:* If using Plague DLC mechanics, consider setting to lower for first run
- *Mod game rules:* Each major mod has its own game rules (Carnalitas frequency, VIET event density, RICE region triggers)
- *Gender equality:* Set to default (historical) for first run; change for challenge runs

#heading(level: 2)[Ruler Selection in the Modded World]

The modlist changes the world significantly. Compared to vanilla:

- *More characters:* Portrait diversity (EPE) and cultural flavor (CFP) make every ruler visually distinct
- *More events:* VIET fires events every year. You won't have quiet decades.
- *More dynamic AI:* More Interactive Vassals means your vassals will scheme, demand, and negotiate
- *Richer regions:* RICE regions (Sahel, Siberia, Tibet) have unique content. Consider playing one of these for a fresh experience.

*Recommended starter for modded:* Same as tutorial (Dublin or Matilda di Canossa), but with richer world. The modlist makes the game more immersive, not harder. Learn the new content on familiar ground.

